Okay, I've just finished tweaking the lighting parameters in index.html to get a brighter, more vivid look (increasing hemi intensity, fog density, tone‑mapping exposure, etc.). The screenshot I captured via the new `/snap` endpoint confirmed that the scene now looks much better overall. However, when I inspect the rendered image, I notice that the ground‑cover “blob” objects (the low‑poly foliage patches) appear a bit too dark and their shadows look overly harsh, especially given the brighter ambient lighting I've just introduced.

I recall that the blob material was originally defined as:

```js
const blobMat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x2e4418, roughness: 1 });
injectSway(blobMat, 0.12);
const blobsPer = 4;
const blobs = new THREE.InstancedMesh(blobGeo, blobMat, treePositions.length * blobsPer);
blobs.castShadow = true; blobs.receiveShadow = true;
```

Two things stand out:

1. **Color** – The hex value `0x2e4418` is a very dark, almost blackish green. With the new lighting, this makes the blobs look muddy and they don’t stand out against the terrain. A slightly lighter, more saturated green would blend better with the brighter environment. I think `0x4a6428` (a medium‑dark olive) will give the foliage a healthier appearance without being neon.

2. **Shadow reception** – The blobs are set to `receiveShadow = true`. Since they are already tiny instanced meshes meant to simulate ground cover, having them receive shadows adds extra dark patches that now look out of place with the increased ambient light. Disabling `receiveShadow` will let them stay lit by the overall scene lighting, reducing the heavy contrast and making the ground look more natural.

I should make these changes directly in the `index.html` file where the blob material is defined. I’ll replace the old material definition block with a new one that updates the color and removes the `receiveShadow` assignment, while keeping everything else (roughness, sway injection, instancing, castShadow) unchanged.

Thus, the edit will replace the exact old string (the block of code above) with the new string reflecting the updated color and the removal of `receiveShadow`. This targeted replacement will adjust the visual appearance of the blobs without affecting any other part of the scene.

